  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 224 AM MST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nAs of 2:15AM, water vapor satellite imagery shows a longwave trough \naxis near the Wyoming/Idaho border. High clouds within southwest \nflow ahead of the trough will shift east with the trough through the \nmorning. Clearing skies will allow for a cold Friday morning for \nmost with temperatures ranging from the mid 20s to mid 30s in the \n6AM to 8AM time frame. Although there will be plenty sunshine today, \ntemperatures will be similar if not slightly cooler compared to \nThursday due to north/northwest flow behind the aforementioned \ntrough. Anticipate high temperatures to be in the mid 40s for \nlocations west of the Continental Divide and the low 50s east of the \nContinental Divide. Winds are overall expected to remain light; \nhowever, occasional breezes around 20 mph are forecast for South \nPass, Dubois, and high elevations of the Absarokas this afternoon. \n\nThere is high confidence that a ridge will begin to build across the \nIntermountain West late tonight and be overhead through at least \nSunday morning. With deep high pressure building into the area, \nanticipate Saturday's highs to be similar to slightly warmer compared \nto today. The only minor thing of note for Saturday will be a weak \nvorticity maximum ahead of the ridge axis. There currently looks to \nbe just enough mid-level moisture associated with the vorticity \nmaximum to support isolated virga showers across northwestern and \nnorthern Wyoming late Saturday morning through early Saturday \nafternoon. There is less than a 10% chance that any of this will \nreach the ground.\n\nThe upper level ridge begins to fold over on Sunday, leaving zonal \nflow aloft across the area. Sunday is forecast to be the warmest day \nout of the next 7 days due to gusty winds ahead of a weak Sunday \nevening cold front and warm 700mb temperatures. Mean 700mb \ntemperatures for Sunday afternoon currently range from 3C to 5C \nacross western and southern Wyoming to 7C to 10C across central and \nnorth-central Wyoming. This translates to highs in the 50s to low \n60s. The warmest temperatures are forecast to occur in Johnson \nCounty with highs around 70 degrees. These highs are around 20 \ndegrees above normal. Given these much above normal temperatures and \ngusty winds on Sunday afternoon, elevated fire weather conditions \nare possible across Johnson County.\n\nThe aforementioned weak cold front is currently forecast to have \nlittle to no moisture associated with its passage late Sunday. The \nonly impact will be slightly cooler temperatures for Monday. \nEnsemble guidance is in consensus that a shortwave and its \nassociated cold front will trek across the area Monday night into \nearly Tuesday, bringing very low precipitation chances to northwest \nWyoming. An upper level ridge looks to build back into the region \nfor Wednesday and Thursday, leading to more dry and above normal \ntemperatures.",
